WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.680
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:08.389
Let me tell you about a beautiful and very
real 19th century love story involving a spellbound

00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:14.440
couple in love, a quaint little barn in the
English countryside and a bunch of body parts

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.219
sold at auction.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:19.130
Well I never said it was a love story with
a happy ending.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:25.740
In 1827 at 22 years of age William Corder
of Polstead, a sleepy English village in Suffolk

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:33.680
planned to elope to that most classic of elopement
locations, Ipswich, with Maria Marten, the

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.170
daughter of a local mole catcher.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.600
They had planned to meet in the middle of
the night, at the local Red Barn, so named

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.070
because of its red roof.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:50.850
And so Maria met Corder in the Red Barn that
night and she was never seen alive again.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.050
Corder fled Polstead without talking to anyone.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.990
I know, he didn’t even leave a note saying
I’ve just slaughtered my girlfriend in that

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.400
creepy looking barn, the cheek of it!

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.390
Maria’s remains were later discovered buried
in a grain storage bin at the Red Barn.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.680
So I think the elopement went really well.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:14.030
Corder evaded capture for some time but he
was eventually tracked down 90 miles away

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.490
in Brentford, London, where he had managed
to find a new lucky wife and was running a

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:26.190
ladies boarding house, the natural career
progression for any aspiring young psychopath.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:31.620
After his capture the local newspapers reported
that he was found “…in parlour with 4

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:37.070
ladies at breakfast, in dressing gown &amp; had
a watch before him by which he was 'minuting'

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.030
the boiling of some eggs.”

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:44.190
Let the man finish boiling his goddamn eggs
before slapping the cuffs on him, that’s

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.130
just downright rude.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:52.780
But something about this murder and boiled
egg fanatic caught the public’s attention

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.430
and in a big way.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:02:00.270
The media all over Britain were reporting
of William Corder’s story of elopement,

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:07.650
betrayal, murder, red barns and eggs, those
poor overcooked eggs.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:13.980
The public went wild for it, they wrote songs
and performed plays of the infamous red barn

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.099
murder, as it has come to be known.

00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:21.040
Well I guess we all needed something to occupy
our days with before Jeremy Kyle brought a

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.310
daily dose of inbreeding and social disfigurement
into our homes.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:31.730
Now when I say the public went wild for this
unfortunate tale, I mean to say they behaved

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:35.239
like a pack of wild priests in a boarding
school.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:41.409
The red barn itself was raided and completely
emptied by the public in search of souvenirs

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:43.060
from the crime scene.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:48.049
Even the wooden panels from the floor and
walls were prised off and sold.

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:52.569
You can imagine it now “murder planks, get
your murder planks, only a shilling a piece,

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:54.870
the blood’s still warm folks”.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.159
A novel was published about the case which
quickly became a best-seller.

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:03.919
Corder was hanged and immediately afterwards,
bits of the rope used to hang him were sold

00:03:03.919 --> 00:03:09.660
to the public and most gruesome of all, his
body parts were chopped up and auctioned to

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:16.590
the highest bidders, not for medical purposes,
nope, just disturbing souvenirs.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.870
Corder’s skin was even used to bind a book,
as can be seen here.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:25.489
Some say afterwards the book grew hair.

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:30.540
All throughout the 18th and 19th centuries
plays were frequently performed detailing

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.230
the red barn murder.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:33.230
Why?

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.559
Am I crazy for thinking that’s crazy?

00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:41.950
Why did the public get so deeply enthralled
with this case?

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:48.209
Overnight William Corder had been raised to
celebrity or even rockstar status, yet Corder

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:55.050
was by all accounts a manipulative, lying,
cheating, abusive, meglomanic psychopath who

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.549
enjoyed chopping up women and hiding them
inside piles of grain.

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:05.199
His only redeeming feature was he cared deeply
about the doneness of a boiled egg.

00:04:05.199 --> 00:04:10.949
In a perfect society should we not forget
about such an evil character, confining them

00:04:10.949 --> 00:04:17.239
to the annals of history and not give the
case so much attention, thereby only glorifying

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:20.739
the horrible act that the killer performed?

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:28.300
Yet we do quite the opposite, we all do, we
are all obsessed with the sinister and macabre,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.250
stop trying to hide it, you are too.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:36.020
I’m just as guilty as the next man of cracking
open a beer and enjoying a Netflix documentary

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:40.129
about the next serial killer who may or may
not be guilty.

00:04:40.129 --> 00:04:47.500
Why are novels, films and TV shows about killers
so much more popular than your average romcom?

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:52.330
Even in a world where romantic filmmakers
could actually write, I still believe this

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.040
would hold true.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:05:01.060
Face it the sick general public absolutely
loves a good gruesome murder story and the

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:06.160
more kills the perpetrator has to their name
the more we lap it up like a sadistic, mentally

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.220
ill kitten.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:13.400
And this has been the case for as long as
man with long beard and stubby nose hit man

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.930
with beady eyes over the head with big rock,
spilling red goo that man can’t drink all

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.710
over the cave floor.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:27.060
Less than 150 years ago Jack the Ripper murdered
and disemboweled anywhere from five to eleven

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.250
prostitutes in Whitechapel, London.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:35.620
Yet today he is much as a household name as
Jesus or Cillit Bang.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:41.160
There are many Jack the Ripper fan clubs out
there, hundreds of books, plays, songs, TV

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.240
shows and films.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:48.259
All because a crazy man down a back alley,
once sliced open some women to see what was

00:05:48.259 --> 00:05:49.470
inside.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:54.569
The ironic thing is Jack the Ripper knew how
sick the general public were and how famous

00:05:54.569 --> 00:05:59.689
he would inevitably become, because he branded
himself.

00:05:59.689 --> 00:06:05.780
In a letter that may have been written by
the man himself he administered himself the

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.190
moniker of “Jack the Ripper”.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:13.729
I would have gone for Slicy Pete myself, but
I guess it’s a personal preference.

00:06:13.729 --> 00:06:18.009
The stories of these professional psychopaths
are endless.

00:06:18.009 --> 00:06:24.100
Such as Dr. Harold Shipman, nicknamed Dr.
Death, the doctor who murdered over 250 of

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.560
his own patients.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:31.659
H.H. Holmes, the American serial killer who
designed and constructed a purpose built hotel

00:06:31.659 --> 00:06:37.699
including hidden rooms and passageways in
the walls, for the sole purpose of torturing

00:06:37.699 --> 00:06:42.960
and murdering his victims, of which there
were an estimated 200.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:50.490
Luis Garavito, nicknamed The Beast, a Colombian
rapist and serial killer who sexually abused,

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.520
tortured and killed over 138 children.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:07:00.790
And those are just the tip of a very large
and deeply evil iceberg.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:07.469
The Yorkshire Ripper, Ian Brady and Myra Hindley,
Ted Bundy, Gary Ridgway, I could go on and

00:07:07.469 --> 00:07:08.710
on all day.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:16.300
In fact the United States has produced 2,743
serial killers, and the UK is hot on its heels

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.620
with 145 psycho murderers on its list.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:26.180
But have you ever noticed how we glorify these
monsters by giving them cool sounding nicknames

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:32.540
Jack the Ripper, Dr. Death, The Beast, The
Grim Sleeper, Son of Sam, The Green River

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.490
Killer, The Lady Killer, BTK.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:42.039
It’s like Quentin Tarantino and Wes Anderson
had a lovechild and it made a film about serial

00:07:42.039 --> 00:07:43.039
killers.

00:07:43.039 --> 00:07:47.360
You can just picture the beautifully pastel
coloured introductory title cards.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:53.629
But don’t you think it's a bit strange how
we are quicker to give awesome sounding monikers

00:07:53.629 --> 00:07:58.930
to mass murdering psychopaths than to our
favourite sports players.

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:04.349
It’s almost like the moment someone disposes
of a few people they automatically have a

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:07.669
god-given right to a cool nickname.

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:09.770
And the general public lap it up.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:15.120
Swiss Psychiatrist Carl Jung believes it's
because we all need to feed our “shadow

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:16.120
self”.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:21.430
Our shadow self is our dark side, that part
of you secretly wants to punch a nun in the

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:25.099
face, even though we know it's wrong.

00:08:25.099 --> 00:08:29.659
We all have it and psychiatrists believe that
by watching serial killers on TV carrying

00:08:29.659 --> 00:08:36.250
out heinous acts and learning how they performed
said acts, we are scratching that itch we

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:42.650
all have to explore our dark side, all from
the safe comfort of our couch.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:47.980
Research shows we find indulging our dark
side, from afar, therapeutic.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:53.180
Greek philosopher Aristotle was curious why
his fellow humans were obsessed with plays

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:57.530
about horrific things like murder.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:00.440
Everyone loved a good tragedy.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:06.950
Aristotle concluded that by experiencing what
he called “dark emotions” such as disgust,

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.630
fear and depravity we can cleanse ourselves
of such emotions.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:15.360
It’s a kind of self healing, he called this
process catharsis.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:21.390
So according to Aristotle, the next time you’re
lying in bed with your wife and that thought

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:27.530
pops into your head again “go on, just put
the pillow over her face, it will only take

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.410
a minute and I’ll be free”.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:36.410
You should instead pop on a gory horror film
or true crime documentary and experience some

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:40.570
good old catharsis to cleanse that evil little
person from your mind.

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:44.120
And if that doesn’t work for you, them I’m
afraid you’re just going to have to start

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.350
planning where to hide the body.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:52.350
Another reason why we find serial killers
absolutely fascinating is because of how perplexing,

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:58.430
difficult and alien the concept of committing
and getting away with multiple murders is

00:09:58.430 --> 00:09:59.430
to us.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:06.090
I’ve never tried it myself but I can imagine
it’s really difficult to murder someone

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:12.560
and not get caught, after all the majority
of murders are solved, approximately two thirds

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.780
of killers are eventually caught.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:21.690
In today’s world of advanced forensics,
ballistics, criminal psychology, CCTV everywhere

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:27.980
and internet and phone tracking, no matter
how smart you think you are, only one tiny

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:33.000
mistake needs to be made before you’re found
out and cuffs have been slapped on your wrists

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.490
faster than you can say “the voices made
me do it”.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:42.410
So naturally we find someone who can avoid
being caught for many years whilst committing

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:48.480
more than one murder, sometimes many many
murders, absolutely fascinating.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.320
After all, that takes some skill.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.940
On some level we find it impressive.

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:01.650
In the same way we find highly skilled acrobatic
skydivers doing twists and turns whilst plummeting

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:07.260
at terminal velocity towards a six-thousand
kilometre rock quite impressive.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:12.450
We feel exhilarated when watching skydivers
because it’s a terrifying experience that

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:19.850
most of us would never dream of doing, yet
we all secretly want to know how it would

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:24.150
feel, so we can live that experience through
watching and learning about somebody else

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.340
doing it on our behalf.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:32.120
In this regard, our fascination with serial
killers is no different.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:37.900
Maybe this psychology also explains why so
many people watch horrendously trashy TV shows,

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:43.160
like Keeping up With The Kardashians and Honey
Boo Boo, just so we can experience through

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:47.280
others what it would feel like to be that
stupid.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:53.900
There’s another deep-rooted evolutionary,
psychological reason for our love of serial

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:55.430
killers.

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:56.960
Uncertainty.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:02.590
Humans hate the unknown, we always have, the
same fear of the unknown can be seen in other

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:03.650
intelligent creatures.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:08.590
It’s why apes at the zoo always seem so
curious of new objects, they’re only interested

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:13.890
because they’re trying to discover if this
new object will hurt them or not.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:19.090
And so too are humans afraid of what may be
around the corner, what is beyond the forest

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.470
or across the ocean.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:27.610
During our 200,000 year span as modern homo
sapiens we have found a way to deal with our

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:31.280
fear of the unknown, we dominate it.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:36.870
We can’t predict what a wild animal will
do when we meet it, it’s actions are unknown,

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:40.740
so we invented weapons to kill them.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:47.100
We were afraid of what dangers may lie across
the ocean, so we invented boats and we took

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:58.180
our weapons and we dominated the oceans and
everything beyond them.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:05.520
Simply because on a deeply psychological level
it terrified us, not knowing what may be out

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:06.520
there.

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:12.870
It has been suggested by many a psychologist
that serial killers kill for the exact same

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:14.360
reason.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:20.810
Because they are afraid, so they feel the
need to dominate their environment.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:27.800
Many have trouble speaking to and becoming
romantically entangled with women, so they

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:33.880
instead choose to dominate them through rape
and murder, it’s no coincidence that an

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:39.660
overwhelming amount of serial killers throughout
history have chosen prostitutes as their targets.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:45.920
Some may have been beaten and abused as a
child so they fear what other humans may do

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:49.730
to them, thus they decide to dominate them.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:55.990
Serial killers often practice this domination
on animals before humans, just as the human

00:13:55.990 --> 00:14:01.380
race first practiced dominating cattle and
sheep, before using the exact same techniques

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.970
to enslave humans during times of war and
famine.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:11.640
As observers we find people dominating others
absolutely fascinating, because it teaches

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:18.260
us how to conquer the unknown, we learn what
we might do if we were put into the same situation

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:20.060
of either the killer or the victim.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:26.320
It’s the same macabre reason why horrific
car crash videos are so popular online, and

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:32.070
fail videos where people have horrendous,
bone-breaking accidents, even if sometimes

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:33.660
they did have it coming.

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:38.390
We aren’t all psychopaths, and although
it makes us feel guilty watching these videos,

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:45.290
all we are doing is feeding a primal urge
to dominate the unknown, deep down, we just

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:49.650
want to answer the question, what if?

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:52.870
What would happen if I was in that situation.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:58.290
And by studying serial killers and enjoying
true crime documentaries, we find out what

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:03.860
happens if we were to go on a satanic killing
spree, and hopefully watching the story of

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:11.470
others unfold before us can dissuade us from
following a similar path, because others are

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:16.240
conquering the unknown for us, whilst we stuff
our faces with popcorn.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:22.800
So thank you serial killers, you are providing
a vital service for all of humanity.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:29.100
No wait, I can’t say that, I can’t actually
thank them, there are victims after all.

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:33.970
Okay how about, serial killers, you are very,
very naughty, but from your naughtiness may

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:44.280
we all learn to be better people, that’s
better, I think.

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:49.720
Thanks for watching.

